# Fingerprints the gitleaks secret-scan job may skip. One line per finding, each
# an exact commit:file:rule:line, so the rule still fails on any other commit,
# file, or line, including a new occurrence of the same pattern. This narrows the
# scan; it never disables a rule, relaxes entropy, or excludes a path.
#
# The entry below is a documentation example in docs/RUNBOOK.md: a verification
# probe that passed curl's user-and-password argument a shell command substitution
# reading one of the paths under infra/secrets/. The curl-auth-user rule reads
# whatever follows that flag as a credential pair, so it matched the substitution
# rather than a password. Nothing was disclosed: the file named there is gitignored
# and has never been committed, which the .gitignore-coverage test in
# tests/test_public_readiness.py asserts.
#
# The probe was rewritten to pass the password on stdin instead, so it stays out of
# the process list on a shared host, which means the line is gone from the tip and
# this commit diff is the only place it survives. The pre-flip history rewrite that
# docs/SECURITY.md already requires for the brand tokens removes it for good, and
# this line goes with it.
#
# This comment describes the pattern rather than quoting it, deliberately. Writing
# the literal here would be a fresh occurrence in a new commit, which the scan
# flags in turn, and answering that with a second fingerprint is a loop that ends
# with an ignore file longer than the finding. tests/test_public_readiness.py
# builds the brand token at runtime for the same reason.
